                "text": "Let me understand the rule and construct 3 demonstration pairs.\n\nThe rule: input grid has orange (7) background, azure (8) bottom row fixed. Colored shapes (non-orange, non-azure) get moved up by their area (number of cells). Each shape connected component of same color.\n\nNote the verifier: it iterates components of same color (not 0, not 7, not 8). Wait, 0 is treated as... actually color != 0 and != 7 and != 8. So shapes are any color that's not 0,7,8. In the example shapes are color 3, 5, 1.\n\nWait, 0 is not background here; background is 7 (orange). Hmm, but the verifier skips 0 too. In this task orange=7. OK.\n\nLet me construct simple grids.\n\nConstraints for verifier to work: moving shape up by its size must keep within grid (nx = x - size >= 0). Otherwise negative index wraps.\n\nLet me design grids.\n\nGrid size 8x8, bottom row all 8. Shapes above, rest 7.\n\nPair 1: a single 3-cell shape. Shape must fit. Let's place a vertical 3-cell shape of color 3 at column 0, rows 4,5,6. Size 3, moves up 3 rows -> rows 1,2,3.\n\nInput:\nrows 0-3: all 7? rows: \nrow0: 7...\nrow1: 7...\nrow2: 7...\nrow3: 7...\nrow4: 3 7 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow5: 3 7 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow6: 3 7 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow7: 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\n\nOutput: shape at rows 1,2,3.\n\nLet's also include multiple shapes with different sizes.\n\nPair 2: two shapes of different colors and sizes.\n\nLet's do color 3 vertical 2 cells (size 2), color 5 vertical 3 cells (size 3).\n\nPlace 3 at col0 rows 6,7? No, row7 is azure. Let's place 3 at col0 rows 5,6 (size 2) moves up 2 -> rows 3,4. Place 5 at col7 rows 4,5,6 (size 3) moves up 3 -> rows 1,2,3.\n\nWait but shapes need to not overlap after move. col0 and col7 separate. Good.\n\nInput rows:\nrow0: all 7\nrow1: all 7\nrow2: all 7\nrow3: all 7\nrow4: 7...7 5 (col7=5)\nrow5: 3 ... 5\nrow6: 3 ... 5\nrow7: 8...\n\nOutput:\n3 moves from rows5,6 to rows3,4 at col0.\n5 moves from rows4,5,6 to rows1,2,3 at col7.\n\nResult:\nrow0: all7\nrow1: col7=5\nrow2: col7=5\nrow3: col0=3, col7=5\nrow4: col0=3\nrow5: 7\nrow6: 7\nrow7: 8\n\nPair 3: shapes with holes / non-vertical shapes, and different sizes. Let's do an L-shape or a horizontal shape.\n\nColor 1 shape size 2 horizontal at row6 cols 3,4. Moves up 2 -> row4 cols3,4.\n\nAlso perhaps color 4 shape size 4 (2x2 square) at rows5,6 cols... wait must avoid azure row. Let's place 2x2 at rows4,5 cols 0,1. Size 4 moves up 4 -> rows0,1 cols0,1.\n\nColor 1 size 2 at row6 cols 3,4 moves to row4.\n\nCheck overlap: none.\n\nInput:\nrow0: all7\nrow1: all7\nrow2: all7\nrow3: all7\nrow4: 4 4 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow5: 4 4 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow6: 7 7 7 1 1 7 7 7\nrow7: 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\n\nNow careful: row6 cols3,4 = 1,1. That's fine.\n\nOutput:\n4 moves (size4) from rows4,5 to rows0,1 cols0,1.\n1 moves (size2) from row6 to row4 cols3,4.\n\nrow0: 4 4 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow1: 4 4 7 7 7 7 7 7\nrow2: all7\nrow3: all7\nrow4: 7 7 7 1 1 7 7 7\nrow5: all7\nrow6: all7\nrow7: 8...\n\nGood.\n\nBut wait, the verifier uses connectivity by same color, 4-connectivity. The 2x2 square is one component. Fine.\n\nLet me submit these three.\n\nActually let me double-check pair 2's shape: color 5 at col7 rows4,5,6 - size 3 vertical, connected. color 3 at col0 rows5,6 size 2. Good. They don't touch.\n\nBut note: in pair 2 input, row4 col7=5, row5 col0=3 and col7=5, row6 col0=3 col7=5. Fine.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input for each.\n\nActually I should verify the verifier output matches my expectation by trusting it. Let's just submit.\n\nLet me provide all three in one block.",
